 4. 🏎️ Elon&#x27;s flying supercar 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Images: Tesla Elon Musk finally unveiled his long-promised next-generation Tesla Roadster last night, and it's every bit as outrageous as you'd expect from a man who launches rockets into space, Axios' Joann Muller writes. 

 The car flies, does backflips and even drives upside down. Why it matters: Musk's latest creation — a collaboration between Tesla and SpaceX engineers — pushes the boundaries of automotive technology, even if it's hard to imagine what practical purpose a flying sports car might serve.

 🚀 Zoom in: At a theatrical unveiling yesterday, Musk showed off a two-seat electric supercar equipped with rocket thrusters and a powerful suction system.

 The Roadster uses high-pressure compressed air, rather than rocket fuel, to propel itself into the air and perform aerial gymnastics. Tesla claims the car can accelerate from zero to 60 mph in just 0.8 seconds — faster than any production car — and will exceed 230 mph, although Musk said the goal is to achieve "half the speed of sound ," or around 380 mph. Screenshot: Tesla Reality check: Tesla hasn't disclosed the Roadster's price, driving range or how long it can stay airborne. Nor has it explained how a rocket-powered flying car could meet safety and regulatory requirements.

