 6. 🐊 Scoop: Trump as Florida Man 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump dances as he walks offstage during an election rally in Kalispell, Mont., on Wednesday. Photo: Alex Wong/Getty Images President Trump will return to his Florida base of operations to juice in-person early voting with an Oct. 23 rally in the southwest part of the state, Axios' Marc Caputo has learned. 

 Why it matters: Florida is among the GOP's least problematic states in midterms, 18 days away. But Republicans are worried about enthusiasm and turnout, even in Trump's newly adopted home state.

 Polls show Rep. Byron Donalds and Sen. Ashley Moody with marginal leads over their Democratic opponents in the gubernatorial and Senate races.

 The Fort Myers rally is in the heart of Donalds' Southwest Florida district, among the state's solid Republican strongholds. 🗳️ State of play: Registered Florida Republicans outnumber registered Democrats by more than 1.5 million.

 So far, though, Democrats have cast more vote-by-mail ballots than Republicans, according to the state's Division of Elections. Republicans used to dominate mail voting in Florida. But that began to change after Trump started demonizing the method in 2020. So now Republicans rely more on early in-person voting.

 "Next week is crunch time," said a top Florida Republican consultant. "It's still Florida, and we still have more voters. But the environment isn't great and, sure, no one turns out our base more than the president. I hope it works." ✈️ Coming attractions: Today, Trump officially kicks off his Florida season by overnighting at his Mar-a-Lago club after a rally this afternoon in Dalton, Ga.

 Tomorrow, he rallies at Northern Kentucky University's arena in Highland Heights, Ky., then is back in Palm Beach for a MAGA Inc. Candlelight Dinner . Sunday, he'll return to the White House. Monday, he rallies in Wichita, Kan. Tuesday, he rallies in Greenville, S.C. Wednesday, he rallies in Clemson, S.C. Thursday, he rallies in Sioux City, Iowa. Next Friday, he returns to Florida for the rally with Donalds and Moody. 
 
